+# Sourced inspiration note
+
+## Parallel cited
+
+A deliberately narrow image-level parallel is *Völuspá* stanza 19 in the Elder Eddas, which names an ash as Yggdrasil and describes water, dew, and greenness around it. The exact stanza locator and delivered excerpt are in `voluspa-19-excerpt.md`. Those are the only factual claims this note makes about the cited passage; it does not assert a traditional symbolic meaning or broader religious practice.
+
+The newly authored `hush-lantern-excerpt.md` uses its own green-glass root and a drop of water as original imagery. This is a visual prompt only: it does not retell the poem, use its characters or settings, or make the Hush Lantern an analogue of Yggdrasil. The external poem is not fictional canon or an in-world source.
+
+## Attribution question
+
+The cited work is *The Elder Eddas of Saemund Sigfusson & The Younger Eddas of Snorre Sturleson*, Benjamin Thorpe's translation of the Elder Eddas (including *Völuspá*), I. A. Blackwell's translation of the Younger Eddas, edited by Rasmus B. Anderson, 1906 edition, Project Gutenberg eBook 14726: <https://www.gutenberg.org/files/14726/14726-h/14726-h.htm>. The cited passage is *Völuspá* stanza 19. This attributes the comparison to that text and translation; no individual ancient author is asserted.
+
+## Rights question
+
+The rights status and applicable reuse terms for this edition and translation have not been independently determined here. Public availability or attribution is not a rights grant; consult the applicable source terms before further reproduction.
+
+## AI-training-permission question
+
+AI-training permission has not been assessed or established. Public accessibility, attribution, and any conclusion about rights do not imply permission for AI training; that question remains separate and unanswered.
